course: dictionary
lesson: 13818
title: Words that mean for ever
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words are long, but the words around them give you clues. «Irrevocably» is one of these. Look at the sentence: «The old bridge was washed away, and the village was cut off irrevocably.» Being washed away and cut off tells you something bad has happened and cannot be undone.
explain: Irrevocably means in a way that can never be changed, cancelled or taken back. To check your meaning, swap it into the sentence: «the village was cut off in a way that can never be changed». If the sentence still makes sense, your meaning is right.
explain: Writers use irrevocably in stories, history and law to show that a moment is final. Once a thing has happened, it stays that way.
fact: Irrevocably means in a way that can never be changed, cancelled or taken back.
fact: «Irrevocably» is an adverb, because it tells us how something happens or is done, as in «changed irrevocably».
fact: «Irrevocably» is a formal word, so you will often find it in stories, history books and law.
fact: When a writer uses irrevocably, they tell the reader that a moment is final.
fact: A good check is to swap your meaning into the sentence and see if it still makes sense.
exercise: «The river changed its course irrevocably after the flood.» What does irrevocably mean? | Very slowly, one step at a time | Quickly and loudly | With a little help from others | *In a way that can never be changed back
exercise: Find the word in «The sign was damaged irrevocably by the storm.» that tells us how it was damaged. | sign | *irrevocably | storm | damaged
exercise: Mia says irrevocably is just another word for sometimes. Why is she wrong? | *It means in a way that can never be changed or taken back | It is the name of a small town | It means very happy and full of joy | It means happening now and then
test: «Once the glass was smashed, the pieces were broken irrevocably.» Irrevocably tells us the pieces | Can be fixed easily with glue | Are very cheap to buy | *Can never be put right | Are easy to see
test: Which swap keeps the meaning of «The decision was made irrevocably»? | The decision was made quietly in a corner | The decision was made after lunch | *The decision was made for good and could not be undone | The decision was made in a hurry, and it was quick
exam: «The painting was damaged irrevocably in the fire.» What does this tell the reader? | The painting was a small picture | The painting was painted again next week | *The painting can never be repaired or restored | The painting was hung on a wall
